# Homelab, session feat-jev-on-gamingpc. Plan approved with yes
# to all three decisions: different timeouts for jev and jev-cpu, one real suspend at the end that I
# run, and R5 gets its own proxy restart. PR 1, the gaming PC replica, merged (homelab #846).
# PR 2 MERGED (homelab #847, 2026-11-03 05:00 UTC), and Flux rolled it out about 05:02: the LiteLLM
# proxy, jev and jev-llm-cpu restarted. jev goes to the gaming PC with timeout 5 and no retries,
# jev-cpu keeps 30 s, the fallback requires agent-d. Its CI had failed on runner egress timeouts.
# Same model, not always the same verdict: 1 of 5 test alerts (NodeFilesystemSpaceFillingUp) was
# incident on the GPU and noise on both CPU backends, which agreed with each other. Not checked
# with make jev-eval: no decisions are labelled yet. Guard p50 before the change: GPU 0.15 s,
# gaming PC 0.70 s, agent-d 1.13-1.34 s. After it, three test calls all reached the gaming PC, no
# fallback, 0.28-0.37 s. The Jev triage pod moved to server-e on its restart, which helps the sleep case.
# JevUnavailable was ALREADY FIRING before the merge, for at least 24 hours, from a separate bug: one
# alert's model answers with severity "warning", Jev rejects that severity with a 503, and
# Alertmanager redelivers it every 10 s. The alert is HomeTileDown, from a service-control tile that
# has failed its probes for about 33 hours. So DONE WHEN's "JevUnavailable stays quiet" cannot be
# judged until that bug is fixed or set aside. That is my call, and nothing owns the fix yet.
# Drills: llama-server frozen with SIGSTOP and resumed with SIGCONT (non-interactive sudo works on
# the gaming PC), a probe every 5 s, three drills in sequence with recovery waits, about 12 minutes.
# R2's idle model server held 0% CPU during them.
# PR 3 is worked out: active_profile off and jev out of pve's catalog, and swap.py scales jev-llm
# down even off the catalog. The pve workflow does not prune, so its Deployment, Service, PVC and PV
# stay at 0 replicas: cleanup goes in the PR body as a step after merge. The PV is Retain, so the
# weights survive. Then the suspend, which I run. R5 waits for PR 3.
#feat/jev-on-gamingpc @forge: This is R4 of the REPLAN in Tasks.herd. Work in Operate mode. You're working in lordmuffin/homelab-gitops on Forgejo. Read docs/jev.md, apps/ai/jev/llm-cpu/, the jev entries in apps/ai/litellm-proxy.yaml, tests/tdd/test_jev_wiring.py, docs/gpu-node-profiles.md and bare-metal/andrew-gamingpc/. Write a short plan and STOP for approval. Goal: Jev's primary runs on the gaming PC's CPU, so pve's card is free, and the guard survives the PC being away. Scope: a) Primary: same GGUF pin and llama.cpp build as today, pinned to andrew-gamingpc, CPU only, on a sensible number of threads for a 12-core 7900X. b) Fallback: jev-llm-cpu stays jev's only fallback, off that node and away from lxc-k3s2-agent-a. c) Decide what gaming-on does to this pod. gaming=true:NoSchedule does not evict it, so say whether it scales down, and check it can't hurt a game. d) Prove failover: sleep or stop the node's pod and show how long guard calls wait. timeout is 30 s, and a dead endpoint may hang rather than refuse. pi-durable fails closed, so measure what that costs it. e) Measure guard latency on primary and fallback against today's GPU, before pve changes. Constraints: JevBackends stays true: never a different model as jev's fallback. Every LiteLLM config change restarts the proxy and interrupts clients, so batch this with R5's alias if you can. DONE WHEN: both backends answer, failover is measured, JevUnavailable stays quiet through it, and pve is free to change.

# fix/voice-request. DONE, merged as #311, released in server-v0.37.0 (#312). On the card gemma4-e4b
# passed 73 of 75 and 24 of 25 cases, failing "two steps" 1 of 3 ("Friday at ten" became Saturday the
# 7th), with reply p95 2.51 s and 0 fallbacks. The ask eval and copilot cases did not run. The control
# passed 50 of 75. Result in fast-tier.md, "The voice request, fixed". The line below is the original prompt.
# Before it merged:
# R5 measured today's request on the card through LiteLLM: voice p95 2.46 s, 62 of 75; the variant
# 2.67 s, 71 of 75. It still fails the bar, so this is the step that decides whether gemma4-e4b is used.
# The 3B control runs on the gaming PC's CPU, which also runs Jev's replica: check herd ls first.
#fix/voice-request @forge: This takes R2's voice-eval findings into the server. Work in Build mode and follow TDD.md. You're working in lordmuffin/jarvis-voice. Read R2's and R5's sections of docs/plans/fast-tier.md, server/src/jarvis_live/voice/, the ProposalIn schema and build_payload, how IdeasState stores and dedupes proposals, and the named-day+kinds+repo variant in server/tests/evals/voice.py. Goal: the request the server sends for voice stops causing the faults every model hit, so a model can pass R2's committed bar on the request live actually sends. Scope: a) Today names its weekday in voice_user, and in the ask path if it sends a date. b) Proposals follow each kind's real shape, so the grammar can no longer produce a git.pr_comment with no body or a reminder with no title. The copilot shares action kinds: say what it shares before splitting a schema, and keep the copilot's request unchanged unless its 17 evals still pass on the change. c) The repo rule: a listed repo only if the request names it or says what it is for, otherwise null. The order of the repo list must not decide it. d) The repeat: gemma4-e4b re-filed an already-proposed task with a due date nobody said, and dedupe missed it because the fields differ. Fix it in the server, deterministically: a voice proposal that adds nothing the speaker said is a duplicate of the stored one. State the rule for what counts as said, and test its edges, including a repeat with a newly spoken date, which is not a duplicate. e) Make the voice eval's default request the server's new one, keeping today's old request and the variants reproducible by name. f) Rerun the voice eval against the bar committed in af416ec, unchanged: gemma4-e4b on the real card, through the interactive alias as lab will call it, reading LiteLLM's response headers to show no attempt fell back to heavy, with reply p95 against 2.5 s; and the q25-3b control on local CPU. If gemma4-e4b passes voice, run R6's ask eval (server/tests/evals/test_ask_evals.py) on the card the same way, and the 17 copilot cases at three runs each, reporting the 11 bar cases. Constraints: the bar does not change. No protocol change unless it must; if so, fixtures and server and Swift tests in the same PR. Models by SHA256, images by digest, no Flash. On the card, one eval at a time and no load test: nothing else calls interactive yet, and its pod has two slots. The gaming PC runs Jev's replica: check herd ls before a CPU run. Eval rows are never committed. This changes what the server sends once voice is on, so title it feat(server). Lab first: voice is off on live. One PR, left for me to merge. DONE WHEN: the PR is open with tests passing, and the voice eval's result on the new request is reported for gemma4-e4b on the card (pass or fail, p95, fallbacks) and for the control, with a one-line recommendation on VOICE_MODEL that I decide.

# R5. DONE 2026-11-03 (homelab #849, #853, #854, #856; docs #299). gemma4-e4b serves alias
# interactive on pve's 2070, thinking off, timeout 5, no retries, heavy as its only fallback. About
# 3.1 GiB on the card (CUDA context about 112 MiB), flat over an hour. Through LiteLLM, 222 of 222
# requests served by interactive: voice p95 2.46 s and 2.67 s (the variant), ask p95 0.72 s. Voice
# reaches 2.60 s with an ask beside it. Quality unchanged from R2, so VOICE_MODEL stays empty.
# Left open: the first request after a pod restart took 27.8 s and fell back (feat/interactive-warmup);
# an auth finding on the proxy, kept out of this public file (fix/litellm-proxy-keys); Whisper
