# Task 11: LLM PATH STALLS: FIND THE CAUSE AND FIX IT (priority: URGENT)
# Depends on: nothing. Blocks: the whole phone track.
# NOT DONE. CAUSE REPRODUCED, PROBE AND FINDINGS MERGED (#236, #239), do not restart this. herd would
# build a fresh worktree for a branch already merged. Prompt below kept for reference.
# Findings: docs/plans/llm-path-latency.md. One long prompt freezes every stream: 8192-token chunks
# at 2,420 tok/s give a 3.4 s step. Idle 0 of 550 stalled. LiteLLM adds about 6.5 ms. The server-d
# network fault looks separate and is unexplained.
# Normal load (#239, 97 gateway requests, short of the 200 asked for): the SLO fails on every
# measure, direct and gateway alike. Gateway short first text p95 96.73 s against 0.8 s, errors 4 of
# 97. The largest wait is the queue for a place: 7.4 of 8 in use, requests waiting in 90% of samples.
# Step 3 cannot reach the SLO alone, because a smaller chunk does not give a queued request a place.
# Voice arm (#252): gate failed, target missed anyway, see PR MERGED below. Steps left: step 1's rerun.
# Step 2 is task 21. Step 3 needs my approval, now the reserved path is decided. Steps 5 and 6 are
# task 12. Step 4, priority, comes last, after R9.
#fix/llm-path-stalls @forge: This is task 11 in Tasks.herd. Work in Operate mode. You're working in lordmuffin/jarvis-voice, but the fix almost certainly lands in lordmuffin/homelab-gitops on Forgejo (the source of truth; GitHub np-home-homelab is only a mirror). Read apps/ai/litellm-proxy.yaml, apps/ai/litellm-proxy-callbacks-configmap.yaml, bare-metal/dgx-spark/models.yaml and docs/dgx-spark-models.md there, then server/src/jarvis_live/llm/client.py here. Write findings to docs/plans/llm-path-latency.md and STOP for approval before changing anything shared. Every homelab service uses this proxy. Goal: a short reply on the Spark arrives in about a second, every time, not just on average. An intermittent 5 second freeze is what makes voice unusable. Context you can trust (measured 2026-10-31, small samples, thinking off, on qwen3.8-flash-next, run from inside the cluster, so not a port-forward artefact): - 12 interleaved pairs with an identical prompt: direct to vLLM first text median 0.39s, max 0.47s total median 1.33s, 1 of 12 stalled (5.2s) through LiteLLM first text median 0.40s, 1 of 12 at 4.3s total median 1.66s, 5 of 12 stalled (6.1-7.6s) - In that run most stalls came AFTER the first text. In other runs first text itself arrived at a suspiciously steady 5.5s, and one request took 25s. - A jarvis-live pod sees the same behaviour. - 12 shorter requests (short prompt, max_tokens 60) through LiteLLM did not stall at all. Unexplained, and possibly the best lead here. - vLLM had 1-3 other requests running throughout, KV cache 16-40%. What this rules out and what it does not: first-token time is healthy, so the headline problem is the stall, not constant overhead. Stalls also occurred direct to vLLM, once, so LiteLLM is not proven to be the only cause and replacing it is not yet a justified fix. Scope: a) A repeatable probe, committed: interleaved direct and via-gateway requests with identical bodies, recording per-chunk arrival times, not only first and total, so a late final chunk is told apart from a mid-stream freeze. Sample vLLM running and waiting counts beside each request. b) Run it enough to be honest: at least 200 pairs, across idle and busy periods, with the short and the long prompt. Report p50, p95, p99. c) Localise the stall. Test these one at a time, treat all as unproven: - other traffic on the shared model (chunked prefill, batching with long-context agents); does direct stall track concurrent load? - the page-cached PLE table that Flash mmaps, and cache pressure on it - LiteLLM pre-call checks and router model-info lookups, including the ModelNotMappedError tracebacks in its log - the two custom callbacks, especially the streaming iterator hook - retries or a fixed timeout (a steady 5.5s smells like one) - the proxy pod itself (762Mi used of a 1Gi limit, single worker?) d) Fix the cause, smallest reversible change first, one change per PR, with the probe's before and after numbers in each PR. e) Propose a real SLO. Starting point: p95 first text under 0.8s and p95 under 2.5s for a 1-2 sentence reply, measured through the gateway while the model carries normal agent load. f) If load on the shared model is the cause, say so. Then the question is priority or a reserved path for interactive callers, not the gateway. Constraints: Forge's triage order applies, diagnose before fixing. Do not lower gpu_memory_utilization or touch the Flash serving args without a stated rollback; a flip costs an 8-13 minute cold load. All model calls stay on the homelab. Feed the probe's metrics into task 9 rather than building a second dashboard. If LiteLLM truly cannot meet the SLO, stop and hand evidence to 13. DONE WHEN: the stall has a named, reproduced cause, and the probe shows the agreed SLO met through the gateway over 200 pairs under normal load.

