#fix/litellm-proxy-hardening @forge: This is task 22 in Tasks.herd. Work in Operate mode. You're working in lordmuffin/homelab-gitops on Forgejo. Read findings 3, 6, 7 and 8 of REVIEW.md in lordmuffin/jarvis-voice, then apps/ai/litellm-proxy.yaml and how CoreDNS is deployed. Write findings to docs/plans/litellm-proxy-hardening.md in jarvis-voice and STOP for approval. Every homelab service uses this proxy. Goal: keeping LiteLLM stops being the option with no costs written down, and the next 5 s DNS stall does not happen. Context you can trust (2026-10-31, read only, not re-run): one of three CoreDNS replicas runs on andrew-gamingpc, which sleeps unpredictably, and PR #832 banned the proxy from that node for that reason. Reloader restarts the pod on every ConfigMap change, grace is 30 s with no preStop, and aliases allow 300 s, so each config PR kills long agent streams. Memory peaked at 865 Mi of a 1 Gi limit in 7 days, with no alert and no OOM kill found. The proxy requests 100m CPU on agent-d, which also takes Forgejo CI bursts. 12 HTTP 500s in 75 minutes came from read timeouts and MidStreamFallbackError, 10 of them from pi-durable. Scope: a) Keep CoreDNS off andrew-gamingpc, one affinity rule, in its own PR. b) One change window, with Task 11's probe run either side: raise the memory limit first, then the CPU request and the read timeout behind the 500s. Find out whether direct would have survived those requests, which nobody knows. c) Placement follow-up: check the physical host map in the private homelab repo and say whether agent-d really is a different failure domain. A new node name is not one. Say why server-c stayed at 3.8 retransmits/s while the others fell. Failover lands on agent-a, -b or -c, which were never measured. A node failure waits out the default 300 s eviction with nothing paging. d) A runbook entry answering: if the stalls return next Tuesday, what does the on-call check first? e) I own LiteLLM upgrades. Propose a trigger and a cadence. The pin is 1.104.2, upstream is 1.106.6, and the package has a supply-chain incident on record. The decision is mine. Constraints: batch config changes, since each one interrupts every client. One change per PR, with probe numbers before and after. Environment-private values stay in the homelab repo, and this repo is public. DONE WHEN: no CoreDNS replica can land on andrew-gamingpc, the window is done with before and after numbers, and the runbook and upgrade proposal are committed.

